            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Spanish for the belt, then the Spanish for to fasten."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: el pinchazo",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "el pinchazo — \"the puncture, the flat tyre\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Tuvimos un pinchazo cerca del pueblo. We had a puncture near the village."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "In Mexico people say la ponchadura."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "pinchazo is built on pinchar \"to prick\". The origin of pinchar is not settled; it is often linked to Latin punctum \"a prick\"."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "One more word for telling, explaining and describing at B1."
